l site.</p><p>By default, when you enable this module, one entity is created from configuration. This is why we call them Config Entities. Marvin, the paranoid android, is created in the database when the module is enabled.</p><p>You can view a list of Robots here. You can also use the \'Operations\' column to edit and delete Robots.</p>','','8.1.7'),(9085,'Robot','','8.1.7'),(9086,'Floopy','','8.1.7'),(9087,'Add robot','','8.1.7'),(9088,'<p>The Pager Example shows you how to use the table and pager render elements\n        along with the PagerSelectExtender provided by the database system. These\n        items together allow you to present paginated tables.</p>\n    <p>In order to see this in action, make sure there are some nodes present in\n        your site. You can use the devel module to add some if needed, or just\n        create them.</p>\n    <p>The table on this page will show you two nodes per page.</p>','','8.1.7'),(9089,'NID','','8.1.7'),(9090,'next','','8.1.7'),(9091,'last','','8.1.7'),(9092,'Generate a list of all entries in the database. There is no filter in the query.','','8.1.7'),(9093,'Id','','8.1.7'),(9094,'uid','','8.1.7'),(9095,'Surname','','8.1.7'),(9096,'Age','','8.1.7'),(9097,'Add entry','','8.1.7'),(9098,'Update entry','','8.1.7'),(9099,'Advanced list','','8.1.7'),(9100,'The Field Example provides a field composed of an HTML RGB value, like #ff00ff. To use it, add the field to a content type.','','8.1.7'),(9101,'JavaScript Example Information','','8.1.7'),(9102,'JavaScript weighting in action','','8.1.7'),(9103,'jQuery UI accordion','','8.1.7'),(9104,'Show list weighting through JavaScript.','','8.1.7'),(9105,'jQuery UI accordion demo.','','8.1.7'),(9106,'the block admin page','','8.1.7'),(9107,'The Block Example provides three sample blocks which demonstrate the various block APIs. To experiment with the blocks, enable and configure them on @block_admin_link.','','8.1.7'),(9108,'This example will search Drupal\'s core folder and display a count of the PHP files in it.','','8.1.7'),(9109,'This can take a while, since there are a lot of files to be searched.','','8.1.7'),(9110,'We will search filesystem just once and save output to the cache. We will use cached data for later requests.','','8.1.7'),(9111,'<a href=\"@url\">Reload this page</a> to see cache in action.','','8.1.7'),(9112,'You can use the button below to remove cached data.','','8.1.7'),(9113,'%count files exist in this Drupal installation; @retrieval in @time ms. <br/>(Source: <span style=\"color:@color;\">@source</span>)','','8.1.7'),(9114,'calculated by traversing the filesystem','','8.1.7'),(9115,'actual file search','','8.1.7'),(9116,'Explicitly remove cached file count','','8.1.7'),(9117,'File search caching','','8.1.7'),(9118,'A cache item can be set as CACHE_PERMANENT, meaning that it will only be removed when explicitly cleared, or it can have an expiration time (a Unix timestamp).','','8.1.7'),(9119,'Cache item does not exist','','8.1.7'),(9120,'Current status of cache item \"cache_example_expiring_item\"','','8.1.7'),(9121,'Any cache item can be set to only expire when explicitly cleared, or to expire at a given time.','','8.1.7'),(9122,'CACHE_PERMANENT','','8.1.7'),(9123,'Immediate expiration','','8.1.7'),(9124,'10 seconds from form submission','','8.1.7'),(9125,'1 minute from form submission','','8.1.7'),(9126,'5 minutes from form submission','','8.1.7'),(9127,'Time before cache expiration','','8.1.7'),(9128,'Create a cache item with this expiration','','8.1.7'),(9129,'Cache expiration settings','','8.1.7'),(9130,'Remove items from the \"cache\" bin that have expired','','8.1.7'),(9131,'Remove all items from the \"cache\" bin regardless of expiration','','8.1.7'),(9132,'Remove all items in the \"cache\" bin with the tag \"cache_example\" set to 1','','8.1.7'),(9133,'Type of cache clearing to do','','8.1.7'),(9134,'Clear or expire cache','','8.1.7'),(9135,'Expire and remove options','','8.1.7'),(9136,'We have APIs to expire cached items and also to just remove them. Unfortunately, they\'re all the same API, cache_clear_all','','8.1.7'),(9137,'\n        <h2>The Tour:</h2>\n        <p>Click the \'Tour\' icon in the admin menu bar to start.</p>\n        <div class=\"button\" id=\"tour-id-1\">First item.</div>\n        <div class=\"button\" id=\"tour-id-2\">Second item.</div>\n        <div class=\"button\" id=\"tour-id-3\">Third item.</div>\n        <div class=\"button\" id=\"tour-id-4\">Fourth item.</div>','','8.1.7'),(9138,'\n<h2>About Tours</h2>\n\n<p>The Tour module allows you you make instructional tours of user interfaces.</p>\n\n<p>The Tour module comes with Drupal 8 and makes it easy for developers to add\n&quot;Tours&quot; for guiding users through unfamiliar user interfaces.</p>\n\n<p>Each tour is comprised of a series of tooltips that provide contextual\ninformation about an interface. The user can start a tour by clicking the\n&quot;Tour&quot; icon on the right side of the Drupal 8 toolbar. The tour icon is only\nvisible when there is a tour available on the current page.</p>\n\n<p>The Tour module provides the Tour API, which makes it easy for developers to\nadd tours to their modules. In most cases, adding a tour is as simple as\ncreating a YAML file in the config directory in their module, containing the\nexpected data. For a detailed example of such a file, see\nconfig/tour.tour.tour-example.yml.</p>\n\n<p>If you are interested in building tours through a user interface, you may\nwant to look at the Tour UI module: <a href=\"https://drupal.org/project/tour_ui\">https://drupal.org/project/tour_ui</a></p>\n\n<p>The Tour module uses the Joyride jQuery plugin for its underlying\nfunctionality. You can find more information about Joyride at\n<a href=\"https://github.com/zurb/joyride\">https://github.com/zurb/joyride</a></p>','','8.1.7'),(9139,'Bookmark','','8.1.7'),(9140,'Flag Bookmark list','','8.1.7'),(9141,'Bookmarked','','8.1.7'),(9142,'Ops','','8.1.7'),(9143,'My Bookmarks','','8.1.7'),(9144,'No bookmarks available.','','8.1.7'),(9145,'Following','','8.1.7'),(9146,'Flag Followers','','8.1.7'),(9147,'Followers','','8.1.7'),(9148,'You are not following any users. :-(','','8.1.7'),(9149,'Flag Followers Content','','8.1.7'),(9150,'Reading List','','8.1.7'),(9151,'Those you follow have not posted any content. :-(','','8.1.7'),(9152,'This page lists the sandwich plugins we\'ve created. The sandwich plugin type is defined in Drupal\\plugin_type_example\\SandwichPluginManager. The various plugins are defined in the Drupal\\plugin_type_example\\Plugin\\Sandwich namespace.','','8.1.7'),(9153,'@id (calories: @calories, foobar: @foobar )','','8.1.7'),(9154,'We have altered this in the alter hook','','8.1.7'),(9155,'Ham, mustard, rocket, sun-dried tomatoes.','','8.1.7'),(9156,'Enjoy Italian style meatballs drenched in irresistible marinara sauce, served on freshly baked bread.','','8.1.7'),(9157,'<h2>PHPUnit for Drupal: A very basic how-to.</h2>\n\n<h3>How to use this example module</h3>\n<p>You really should be reading the various docblocks in the test files.</p>\n\n<h3>How To:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><p>PHPUnit tests belong in their own directory, so they won&#39;t be loaded\n  by the autoloader during normal bootstrap. This means you should have a\n  <code>/tests/src</code> directory in the root of your module directory.</p>\n</li>\n\n<li><p>Your tests should be in the <code>Drupal\\Tests\\[your_module]\\Unit</code>\n  namespace. Under Drupal&#39;s PSR-4 system, this means your PHPUnit-based\n  tests should go in a <code>[your_module]/tests/src/Unit</code>\n  directory.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Your test case should subclass <code>Drupal\\Tests\\UnitTestCase</code>.\n   </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n  <p>You can run PHPUnit-based tests from within Drupal 8 by enabling the\n  Testing module and then selecting the PHPUnit group from the testing page.\n  As of this writing, this method doesn&#39;t provide any useful output.\n  </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Standard PHPUnit Practices</h3>\n<p>You can (and really, should) run PHPUnit from the command line.</p>\n<p>On unix-based systems this means you need to <code>cd core</code> and then\n  <pre><code>./vendor/bin/phpunit</code></pre>\n</p>\n<p>On Windows-based systems, assuming you have php in your path,\n  <pre><code>php ./vendor/phpunit/phpunit/composer/bin/phpunit --group phpunit_example</code></pre>\n</p>\n<p>Also, you should mark your tests as belonging to a group, so they can be run\n   independently. You do this by annotating your test classes with\n   <code>@group group_name</code>. You should have a <code>@group</code> for\n   your module name, and you should also have a <code>@group</code> for\n   integrations, such as <code>views</code>.\n</p>\n<p>So, for instance, to run all of the PHPUnit example tests, you would type\n  <pre><code>./vendor/bin/phpunit --group phpunit_example</code></pre>\n</p>\n<p>As you can see, including a <code>@group</code> annotation is a good idea.</p>','','8.1.7'),(9158,'Explanation of the language options is found on the <a href=\":languages_list_page\">languages list page</a>.','','8.1.7'),(9159,'Click sections to expand or collapse:','','8.1.7'),(9160,'JS Example: jQuery UI accordion','','8.1.7'),(9161,'\n--\nMail altered by email_example module.','','8.1.7'),(9162,'2Movepeople','','8.1.7'),(9163,'2Movepeople Dashboard','','8.1.7'),(9164,'Error: @message in @file, line @line','','8.1.7'),(9165,'Drush command terminated abnormally due to an unrecoverable error.@message','','8.1.7'),(9166,'Klik on each section to expand or collapse the progressions:','','8.1.7'),(9167,'Modal Form Example','','8.1.7'),(9168,'Submit handler: You specified a title of %title.','','8.1.7'),(9169,'You specified a title of %title.','','8.1.7'),(9170,'The title must be at least 5 characters long.','','8.1.7'),(9171,'Demonstrates a database update operation.','','8.1.7'),(9172,'@pid: @name @surname (@age)','','8.1.7'),(9173,'Choose entry to update','','8.1.7'),(9174,'Updated fir